WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.550
MCP capabilities commonly appear as tools, resources, and prompts.

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.845
A tool is a callable operation exposed by a server.

3
00:00:07.845 --> 00:00:16.632
It may look up information, query a system, create a ticket, summarize a file, trigger a workflow, or perform another defined action.

4
00:00:16.632 --> 00:00:26.518
A resource is data or content made available as context, such as a file, schema, record, document, repository item, or application-specific object.

5
00:00:26.518 --> 00:00:31.931
A prompt is a reusable prompt template or interaction pattern exposed by a server.

6
00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:36.717
Tools and resources are easy to confuse, but they serve different roles.

7
00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:40.639
A resource gives the AI application content to reason over.

8
00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:43.621
A tool does something or requests something.

9
00:00:43.621 --> 00:00:49.819
Reading an API schema as context is different from calling an operation that changes a system.

10
00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:53.584
Fetching a document is different from updating a ticket.

11
00:00:53.584 --> 00:01:03.156
Good courseware, documentation, and system design should make those distinctions visible because the risks and approval needs are different.

12
00:01:03.156 --> 00:01:07.863
Prompts are also distinct from user messages and system instructions.

13
00:01:07.863 --> 00:01:16.650
A prompt template may help standardize how a task is framed, but it should not be treated as a replacement for policy or access control.

14
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:22.769
Tool descriptions, resource metadata, and prompt templates all influence model behavior.

15
00:01:22.769 --> 00:01:30.536
They affect what the model thinks a capability is for, when it should be used, and how the result should be interpreted.

16
00:01:30.536 --> 00:01:34.459
That makes capability metadata operationally important.

17
00:01:34.459 --> 00:01:39.323
Capabilities should be clear, narrow, documented, and understandable.

18
00:01:39.323 --> 00:01:47.012
A narrowly described read-only lookup is easier to review than a broad tool that can perform many unrelated actions.

19
00:01:47.012 --> 00:01:54.700
Documentation should explain purpose, owner, inputs, outputs, permissions, data sensitivity, and expected use.

20
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.426
This is not just tidiness.

21
00:01:56.426 --> 00:02:08.195
Clear capability boundaries reduce confusion, make approval decisions easier, and help teams scale MCP adoption without losing track of what each integration can actually do.
